Investigate whether a chemical supply can survive and recover from disruption (@cs-continuity-project: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @chemical-supply-systems, #source "../sources/Chemical-Supply-Chains-and-Disruption-Risk-EPA.lingua#r_6Y999Z6S9P5T6FY75NA2HGVW6C", #source "../sources/Chemical-Supply-Chain-Profiles-EPA.lingua#r_0E09Q6T7CC9HNWZB3N84E7K21A", #source "../sources/Chemical-Supply-Resilience-EPA.lingua#r_2NVE96BRQ8Q3WYFP2ZQ3NTE11F", #source "../sources/Process-Industry-Supply-Chain-Management-Liu.lingua#r_5W74Q34MKEJAWVS30JV4QH01YQ", #source "../sources/Graduate-Supply-Chain-Planning-MIT.lingua#r_16MZ0PJM4KC10A3D9AYYESBT6P", #chemical-supply-systems "A-complete-qualified-chemical-supply-system.lingua#r_44MR9J6NH9MF50YCHKGAT4GMK3") { r_1YMFKJT4EE1WG1J2SCQSCKP2WB
**Read:** [Understanding Water Treatment Chemical Supply Chains and the Risk of Disruptions — EPA](../sources/Chemical-Supply-Chains-and-Disruption-Risk-EPA.lingua#r_6Y999Z6S9P5T6FY75NA2HGVW6C), the complete report and methods; [Water-treatment chemical supply-chain profiles — EPA](../sources/Chemical-Supply-Chain-Profiles-EPA.lingua#r_0E09Q6T7CC9HNWZB3N84E7K21A), complete profiles for the actual chain; [Supply Chain Resilience: Guide for Water and Wastewater Utilities — EPA](../sources/Chemical-Supply-Resilience-EPA.lingua#r_2NVE96BRQ8Q3WYFP2ZQ3NTE11F), the whole guide; [Supply chain management for the process industry — Songsong Liu](../sources/Process-Industry-Supply-Chain-Management-Liu.lingua#r_5W74Q34MKEJAWVS30JV4QH01YQ), complete relevant uncertainty/dynamic planning studies; [Graduate Supply Chain Planning — MIT 15.762J](../sources/Graduate-Supply-Chain-Planning-MIT.lingua#r_16MZ0PJM4KC10A3D9AYYESBT6P), the full applicable graduate work.

Read the full EPA disruption report, companion guide and complete profiles for all consequential materials in the selected chain. Work the relevant graduate and thesis methods in full. Build an evidence-based dependency map linking chemical sources, co-products, terminals, utilities, transport, analysis, equipment and trained work to a user's actual service.

Define disruption and restoration scenarios with explicit physical mechanisms and time histories. Distinguish ordinary variation, shared failures, quality loss and a changing context. Derive probabilities only where justified; otherwise compare plausible scenarios or ranges. Independently reproduce the finite two-source example, then test its assumptions against actual capacities, qualification, substitution, delivery and reserve conditions.

Compare stock placement, qualified alternatives, shortened repair/release time, shared-service independence and demand flexibility. Calculate usable inventory/service throughout interruption and recovery, not just before and after. Include aging, returned material, resources, work and consequences across parties. Separate parameter uncertainty, model discrepancy and computational error.

Produce a substantial reproducible study with alternatives, sensitivity, limits and an independent challenge. Revise the proposal and identify what field evidence would establish actual readiness. A strong scholarly extension should deepen the relationship between common dependencies, technical qualification and restoration; it should not claim guaranteed continuity because a short simulation happened to have no failures.

**Continue:** [Defend one complete chemical supply from origin to use and recovery](A-complete-qualified-chemical-supply-system.lingua#r_44MR9J6NH9MF50YCHKGAT4GMK3).

